WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:03.720
In 1820, French astronomer

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:07.070
Alexis Bouvard noticed something strange.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:08.860
The gravity of the inner planets

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:10.180
couldn't account for the orbital

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:12.873
movement of the outermost planet, Uranus.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.300
His hypothesis: There must be

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.123
a massive object influencing it.

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:21.370
That eventually led mathematicians

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:22.570
to correctly calculate

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:25.313
the location of our
eighth planet, Neptune.

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:28.850
And this discovery fueled a mad hunt

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:31.920
to discover more planets
using this method.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.690
To give it an approximation,

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.250
everybody and their brother

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.780
have looked for planets beyond Neptune.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.450
I mean, the moment Neptune was discovered,

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:42.635
everyone wanted to do it because

00:00:42.635 --> 00:00:44.480
it was this clear demonstration

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.533
that you can use math to discover objects.

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:51.110
Unfortunately, since then,

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:54.380
no major planet in our
solar system has been found.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:56.560
But thanks to modern telescopes,

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.240
thousands of smaller objects

00:00:58.240 --> 00:00:59.400
have been discovered in the past

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.710
few decades beyond Neptune

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.200
called trans-Neptunian objects, or TNOs,

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.750
like Sedna, one nicknamed The Goblin

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:11.350
and another nicknamed Biden.

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:12.900
And planetary scientists

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.330
at the California Institute of Technology

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:18.700
have noticed some anomalies
with these objects.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.150
The first is the fact that the orbits

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:22.860
all lie in the same plane.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.930
The second is that they all swing out

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:27.040
into the same direction.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.603
Now, the third is where
it gets interesting.

00:01:30.739 --> 00:01:32.350
A large fraction of these orbits

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.170
that swing out into what look like

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:37.310
they've been arranged are detached

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:38.863
from the orbit of Neptune.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:42.830
Their hypothesis? A giant theoretical

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:44.560
planet beyond Neptune,

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:45.930
which accounts for the anomalies

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.443
of these objects: Planet Nine.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:51.050
And thanks to a new telescope

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:52.540
equipped with the largest

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.290
digital camera ever built,

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:56.810
we may be one sky survey

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:58.523
away from finally finding it.

00:02:00.717 --> 00:02:03.450
It will be a game changer because it's big

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.390
and it can detect very faint objects.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:07.530
We will find objects all the way

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.530
from near-Earth asteroids out

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:12.113
to trans-Neptunian objects and beyond.

00:02:23.967 --> 00:02:25.837
After the discovery of Neptune,

00:02:25.837 --> 00:02:27.569
scientists thought there had

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:29.510
to be yet another planet to account

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:32.720
for the remaining orbital
anomalies of Uranus.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:35.100
They called it Planet X.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.650
There were hundreds of attempts

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:38.720
by scientists to find this missing planet

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:40.890
over the next few decades.

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:42.750
But as it turns out,

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:43.743
they were wrong.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:46.520
Astronomers had incorrectly

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.500
estimated Neptune's mass,

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.180
later confirmed by Voyager in 1989,

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:54.430
which did account for
these remaining anomalies.

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.020
The reason Pluto was initially

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:57.680
considered a planet is because

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.240
it was an object that was sort of

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:01.640
in the right part of the sky

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:05.270
where Clyde Tombaugh was
looking for Planet X,

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:07.770
a predicted object that was supposed

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:10.833
to resolve remaining
orbital anomalies of Uranus.

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:12.780
Although Pluto would later

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.723
be classified as a dwarf planet,

00:03:14.723 --> 00:03:16.520
since it was too small

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:17.640
and too far away to have

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.890
any influence on the outer planets.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.640
This was the start of a new era

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:22.810
for discoveries of bodies

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:24.323
in the outer solar system.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:26.440
Pluto is one of what's thought

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.123
to be hundreds of thousands of TNOs.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:31.890
And the more that are discovered,

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:34.413
the more data seems to
point to a ninth planet.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.700
We see this grouping of trajectories,

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.410
all these little pieces of debris

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:43.530
that are hanging out in this population

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.900
of icy planetesimals beyond Neptune.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.906
All of their orbits tend to be

00:03:47.906 --> 00:03:50.810
aligned in a very specific way

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:53.310
that is difficult to ascribe to chance

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:55.920
or to describe in any other way

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:59.450
other than the existence of yet another

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:01.298
planetary mass body hanging out

00:04:01.298 --> 00:04:03.023
in the distant solar system.

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:06.270
The aligned orbits hint at

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:09.530
a large body pulling them
in the same direction.

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:10.930
They also found each TNO

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:12.630
to be tilted on its axis

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:14.173
in approximately the same way.

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:17.780
And they don't appear to
be put there by Neptune.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.130
These orbits should all hug

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:23.980
the orbit of Neptune
and many of them don't.

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:24.870
Why not?

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.310
Well, the here in Planet Nine

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:29.460
once again comes back into the fold.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.280
You need Planet Nine's gravity,

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:32.113
as it turns out,

00:04:32.113 --> 00:04:34.430
to kind of untether these objects

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:36.263
from the orbit of Neptune.

00:04:37.983 --> 00:04:41.300
There's other circumstantial
evidence as well.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:44.300
If you compare the sizes
of discovered exoplanets,

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.510
planets outside our solar system,

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:48.280
the majority fall into categories

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:51.370
known as super-Earths or mini-Neptunes.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:54.160
On average, each star,

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:55.930
if you look up in the night sky,

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:57.910
has at least one planet.

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:02.060
And most planets that inhabit this galaxy,

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:04.550
if you will, are super-Earths,

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:07.200
these objects that are a few Earth masses.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.090
It's actually quite remarkable

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:10.800
that the solar system,

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:14.433
as efficient as it was
at creating planets,

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:18.300
appears to be missing these

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.950
short period super-Earth type objects,

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:22.550
which are very common elsewhere.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:26.180
So if Planet Nine does exist

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:28.330
and is super-Earth size,

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:29.610
it would make our solar system

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.733
similar to the majority we've observed.

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:38.810
To make all these calculations,

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:41.630
and determine the potential
orbit of Planet Nine,

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:43.432
the team at Caltech put collected data

00:05:43.432 --> 00:05:45.423
into computer simulations.

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:49.534
As far as the math allows us to calculate,

00:05:49.534 --> 00:05:52.250
it's about five Earth masses.

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:54.450
So it falls in between

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:55.920
an Earth-type planet

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.843
and an ice giant, such
as Uranus or Neptune.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:03.390
They also estimate that

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:07.120
it's on a 20,000-year
orbit around the sun.

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:08.730
That's 10 times further away

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:10.880
than Neptune is from the sun.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:13.280
We can calculate the orbit

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:15.620
and the mass of Planet Nine

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:18.000
to a pretty good approximation.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:19.840
What we cannot do is tell you

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:22.690
where it is on the night sky.

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:25.100
Fundamentally, it's all just gravity.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:28.040
It's a different type of dynamics

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:29.930
which allows us to infer

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:31.540
the existence of Planet Nine

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:33.820
than was the case with Neptune.

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:35.560
With Neptune, you could say

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:38.800
it's right there and get
the orbit kind of wrong.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:41.600
Here you have information about the orbit,

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:43.610
not so much the information

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:44.990
of where it is on the night sky.

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:47.430
So that's why we're stuck kind

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:50.010
of observing a whole swath of the sky,

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:51.100
which takes a long time

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:52.793
and is indeed difficult to do.

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:55.870
That massive swath of sky

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:56.982
makes finding such a faint,

00:06:56.982 --> 00:06:58.900
far away object like finding

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:00.293
a needle in a haystack.

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:04.930
Planet Nine is millions of times

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:08.120
less bright on the night sky than Neptune.

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:10.830
That's really, really dim.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:12.190
I mean, if you really think about it,

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:13.170
Neptune is not something

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:16.530
you can observe with your naked eye.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:18.720
You need a telescope to see,

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.523
and Planet Nine is millions
of times less bright.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:28.370
Critics say that the team at Caltech

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:29.992
have an observational bias,

00:07:29.992 --> 00:07:31.390
having not looked in enough

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:33.270
quadrants of the sky at different

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:34.530
times of year to conclude

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:37.310
that there is in fact orbital clustering.

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:39.703
Essentially, more data is needed.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:41.910
The good news is we are kind

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:44.360
of entering the next generation

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:47.740
of such surveys now with the commissioning

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:48.600
and the construction of

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:51.461
the Vera Rubin telescope in Chile.

00:07:51.461 --> 00:07:53.580
So I think that's going to be

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:56.213
a game changer in this problem.

00:07:58.230 --> 00:07:59.840
The Vera Rubin Observatory

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:01.670
is a new telescope that'll be

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:03.900
coming online in the next couple of years

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:05.540
to complete the widest,

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:08.933
most detailed survey of
the sky ever conducted.

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:13.910
This Legacy Survey of
Space and Time, or LSST,

00:08:13.910 --> 00:08:16.050
may finally tell us once and for all,

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:17.563
if Planet Nine is out there.

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:24.860
Rubin Observatory is a next-generation

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:26.940
survey telescope that is set

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:31.070
to observe the entire
night sky every few nights,

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:33.010
repeatedly for 10 years.

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:34.660
The idea being we can build up

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:36.880
not only a deep image of the sky,

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:39.443
but also a multicolor movie.

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:41.400
Unlike telescopes

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:43.450
like the Hubble Space Telescope,

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:44.670
the Rubin Observatory's

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:47.700
field of view is extremely wide.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:50.630
And thanks to its giant
3-gigapixel camera,

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:53.070
which is the size of a small car,

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:54.610
it can also detect incredibly

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:56.360
faint objects lurking in

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:58.740
the outer regions of the solar system,

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:00.550
so then narrow field of view telescopes

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:02.653
like Hubble can take a closer look.

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:05.770
Because it's a survey telescope,

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:07.000
we have a plan to go

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:09.770
and do this survey across the night sky,

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:12.230
but then also process all the images

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:14.490
and reduce those to catalogs

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:16.490
so that astronomers can come along

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:18.010
and then they can look at the catalogs

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:20.740
and they can do searches
for unusual objects

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:23.100
or things that match
what they're looking for

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:25.350
just using the catalogs
that we're producing.

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:28.850
That'll help scientists
solve the mysteries

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:31.160
of dark matter, dark energy,

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:32.740
map the Milky Way

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:34.350
and inventory moving objects

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:36.323
throughout the solar system like TNOs.

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:40.098
So, for example, in the solar system,

00:09:40.098 --> 00:09:42.270
almost every population of object

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:43.913
that you can think of in the solar system,

00:09:43.913 --> 00:09:48.390
we will detect about 10
to 100 times more of.

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:52.820
We will take 2 million
images over 10 years,

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:55.673
which is actually 4 million actual

00:09:55.673 --> 00:09:57.710
pictures of the night sky.

00:09:57.710 --> 00:10:01.110
So each image is 3.2 gigapixels.

00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:05.483
And we take about 1,600 images per night.

00:10:05.483 --> 00:10:08.483
That's about 20 terabytes
of data per night.

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:14.770
To find objects within our solar system,

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:16.540
the telescope will take two images

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:18.180
of every patch of sky,

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:20.360
separated by an hour.

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:21.260
And from the motion of

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:23.260
the points of light over that hour,

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:24.470
they'll be able to distinguish

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:25.910
between faint stars

00:10:25.910 --> 00:10:28.003
and objects within our own solar system.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:31.180
That's a problem for Planet Nine though.

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:33.270
Objects that are far beyond Neptune

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:34.530
will be difficult to detect

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.910
from the motion of only an hour.

00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:38.900
Another problem is Planet Nine

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:41.610
may be in the Northern Hemisphere sky.

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:43.430
Due to the frequent clear skies

00:10:43.430 --> 00:10:45.300
and low light pollution,

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:47.210
Rubin Observatory is strategically

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:49.550
located below the equator in Chile

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:53.010
and will mostly see
Southern Hemisphere skies.

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:54.590
But if it is there,

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:56.640
Rubin Observatory is equipped to find it.

00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:02.410
I have colleagues who
believe in Planet Nine,

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:03.243
and I have colleagues

00:11:03.243 --> 00:11:05.113
who don't believe in Planet Nine.

00:11:06.204 --> 00:11:07.320
It's really hard to say

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:09.683
definitively that it's out there.

00:11:09.683 --> 00:11:11.300
We have to go and look.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:13.890
So one of the things that LSST

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:16.380
can do is actually contribute

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:18.270
by surveying more of the sky.

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:19.800
So people are already looking

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:20.960
for Planet Nine.

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:22.630
But given the fact that you have

00:11:22.630 --> 00:11:24.570
to look at a large amount of sky

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:26.540
and you need to look for very

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:28.200
faint kind of objects,

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:30.840
that kind of can be a slow process

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:32.302
so LSST will be able to do

00:11:32.302 --> 00:11:33.940
that much more quickly.

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:38.160
So within the first year
of surveying the sky,

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:39.720
LSST could see if there was

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:41.600
a Planet Nine within its survey

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:43.620
footprint down to the magnitudes

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:44.523
that we can see.

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:46.433
The other thing

00:11:46.433 --> 00:11:47.330
though that we can do

00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:49.470
is provide further constraints

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:52.870
on what the orbit could be,

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:54.290
because a lot of the constraints

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:57.595
so far come from these
also relatively rare,

00:11:57.595 --> 00:12:00.450
very distant objects.

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:02.930
And we'll find a lot more of those too.

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:03.950
We'll also be able to tell

00:12:03.950 --> 00:12:05.890
if it really is just a bias

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:07.113
in the observing data.

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:11.920
Even if the planet isn't found,

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:13.910
it may give astronomers more data

00:12:13.910 --> 00:12:16.430
to find it during future surveys.

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:17.630
Or it may show that there are

00:12:17.630 --> 00:12:19.160
other explanations for the orbits

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:20.780
and tilts of these TNOs

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:22.653
as some scientists have argued.

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:26.760
Either way, a hunt to find Planet Nine

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:29.134
will undoubtedly lead
to the location of new,

00:12:29.134 --> 00:12:31.050
undiscovered TNOs

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:33.880
and whatever else is in our neighborhood.

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:36.270
From the perspective of daily life,

00:12:36.270 --> 00:12:38.500
no one's going to change

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:40.160
their daily habits,

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:41.660
you know, start brushing

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:43.360
their teeth more or whatever,

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:44.814
you know, because there's a planet

00:12:44.814 --> 00:12:48.120
hundreds of times as far away

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:50.080
from the sun as is the Earth.

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:51.920
But at the same time, look,

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:55.960
I mean, it is part of
human nature to explore.

00:12:55.960 --> 00:12:59.400
It is part of human
nature to ask the question

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:00.250
what's out there?

00:13:00.250 --> 00:13:02.280
What lies beyond the ocean?

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:05.040
What lies beyond this hill?

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:06.540
What lies beyond this planet?

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:08.933
What lies beyond the
edge of the solar system?

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:12.700
There is something deeply satisfying

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:16.000
about asking and answering these questions

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:18.700
that I think is really important

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:21.683
to kind of our collective
human experience.

